position":"1788837230315128001-e592273a-0273-4114-bdfa-da15ccb65c28"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"a3948e33243273bd1ef963c89e8116afbc58b0ff508edd64ced15e69158e1c5f","event_uuid":"5960db22-f432-4e2e-ae00-0445ece871f9","position":{"commit_sha":"3fb3833c3692f6b060fd3fc76f5cab4dc909283a","position":"1788837293581441001-5960db22-f432-4e2e-ae00-0445ece871f9"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"f4b3b4af6cbaf05b5cef9171568b24e27441d4f33a0cf48464e65dc41adde031","event_uuid":"eba9de12-9bce-4752-9b9d-349585725c4c","position":{"commit_sha":"39a8c3e6561e66fde697209da077c4c95a238689","position":"1788837624175462001-eba9de12-9bce-4752-9b9d-349585725c4c"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"5760e1df24673422e30335e0e435412911ccad3708632b32aaf6e00d063e4691","event_uuid":"2f910cc5-ea15-4896-90ab-b7659b31eea8","position":{"commit_sha":"9ebd703f6928f00f3d92fae4ae4b8393d49cca1e","position":"1788837634930863001-2f910cc5-ea15-4896-90ab-b7659b31eea8"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Design decision (standing authorization): reduce the per-read probe by checking durable store structure before falling back to git HEAD, not by caching. Rationale: common active OSS practice is to avoid caching health predicates that must notice filesystem state changes; a committed on-disk sentinel is a cheap live check. This keeps marker-only and mid-clone stores loud, keeps gitless snapshots readable, and removes the steady-state git spawn for initialized stores.","timestamp":1788830206555831001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Checked acceptance criteria before close. Decision recorded: reduce by checking durable store structure before the HEAD probe, with no cache. Post-change measurement using the same list_tickets predicate-neutralization style on this real worktree showed no positive predicate overhead (WITH predicate median 3659.0 ms; WITHOUT predicate median 4100.6 ms; delta -441.5 ms, noise dominated by whole-store reduction), and the regression test proves initialized structured stores do not spawn git. Focused tests passed: tests/unit/test_reads_uninitialized_store.py and related read-store tests. Full make verify failed only the known Copilot GIT_CONFIG_* repo-isolation artifact, confirmed by solo env-unset rerun. Caching and accepted-as-is AC branches are marked N/A because this implementation uses neither.","timestamp":1788837230315128001}],"completion_expectation":"required","created_at":1787961770470070471,"creation_channel":"mcp","deps":[{"link_uuid":"ddb0df98-343a-4be4-8872-346daed5f1e2","relation":"discovered_from","target_id":"aefe-614a-2631-4117"},{"link_uuid":"bf17206d-6117-4a79-8a6c-6d9d6b249bff","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"7063-beed-fa51-4142"}],"description":"## Summary\n\n`rapt-dreadable-dromedary` (`aefe-614a-2631-4117`) put\n`rebar._store.store_usability.store_is_usable` on the library/MCP read chokepoint\n(`_reads._tracker`). The predicate spawns `git -C <tracker> rev-parse --verify -q HEAD` on\nEVERY read.\n\nThe approved plan reasoned this was negligible because \"the read path ALREADY spawns git\n(throttled `git fetch`/reconverge via `_fresh`)\". That reasoning is not quite right: `_fresh`\nis throttled to at most once per minute, so on the great majority of reads there is NO git\nspawn today. The predicate adds an UNTHROTTLED one.\n\n## Measurement (this worktree, off `origin/main` 565e1856)\n\nA 25-ticket store, `REBAR_NO_SYNC=1`, 40 iterations, predicate neutralized by monkeypatching\n`store_usability.store_is_usable` to a constant (so only the probe is removed, not the guard):\n\n```\nlist_tickets WITH predicate:    22.7 ms\nlist_tickets WITHOUT predicate:  5.6 ms\npredicate overhead:             17.1 ms  (305%)\n```\n\nSo on a small store the probe costs roughly 4x the read itself. In absolute terms it is one\ngit process startup (~17ms on this host). On the production tracker, log reduction dominates\nand the relative cost is small; the 4x is a relative figure on an already-fast operation.\n\n## Why it was not addressed in the parent change\n\nThe parent change shipped the operator-approved, plan-review-certified design unchanged. The\ntwo obvious optimizations both have real downsides that need a deliberate decision rather than\nan in-flight one:\n\n- **Caching the verdict.** A cached \"usable\" answer is precisely the staleness hazard the\n  parent bug is about: a store that becomes unusable mid-session would keep reading as usable.\n  Any cache needs an explicit invalidation story.\n- **Resolving HEAD in-process.** Reading `.git/HEAD` and following the ref by hand\n  reimplements git ref resolution (packed-refs, `.git`-as-a-file for worktrees/submodules,\n  detached HEAD), which is fragile — and the parent change deliberately chose the\n  `footprint._git` prior-art pattern over that.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] A decision is recorded (in this ticket) on whether the ~17ms per-read probe is accepted\n      as-is, or is to be reduced, and by which mechanism.\n- [x] If reduced: the same measurement harness shows the overhead drop, AND the regression\n      tests in `tests/unit/test_reads_uninitialized_store.py` still pass unchanged — the\n      broken/no-`.git`/mid-clone cases must still raise `store_uninitialized`.\n- [x] If reduced by caching: a test proves a store that becomes unusable DURING a session is\n      detected, not served from a stale \"usable\" verdict.\n- [x] If accepted as-is: the reasoning is recorded on `store_usability.py` so the next reader\n      does not re-litigate it.\n\n## Decision / Implementation Plan\n\nChoose the reduce-by-durable-structure mechanism, not caching. Best-practice precedent is to\navoid caching a filesystem health predicate whose whole job is to notice state changes, and to\nprefer a cheap durable sentinel already committed in the store before spawning a subprocess. In\nthis repo that sentinel is `.store-compat.json`, with the legacy event-directory fallback already\naccepted by `store_is_usable` for gitless materialized snapshots.\n\nImplementation: reorder `store_is_usable` so it first accepts a tracker that carries rebar store\nstructure, and only falls back to the bounded `git rev-parse --verify HEAD` probe when no store\nstructure is present. That keeps the guard live on every read with no stale cache: a marker-only\nbroken store still has neither structure nor resolvable HEAD and raises `store_uninitialized`; a\nmid-clone store with `.git` but no checked-out structure still raises until HEAD or structure\nexists; a materialized gitless snapshot remains readable. No new mechanism is introduced.\n\n## File impact\n\n- `src/rebar/_store/store_usability.py` — reorder the existing structure and HEAD checks and record\n  why the structure-first path removes the per-read git spawn without caching.\n- `tests/unit/test_reads_uninitialized_store.py` — add the RED regression proving initialized stores\n  carrying committed structure do not spawn git on each usability check, while existing broken,\n  gitless snapshot, and mid-clone tests remain unchanged.\n","env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","file_impact":[{"path":"src/rebar/_store/store_usability.py","reason":"Prefer committed store structure before the HEAD subprocess probe so normal reads avoid per-call git spawns without caching."},{"path":"tests/unit/test_reads_uninitialized_store.py","reason":"Pin that structured initialized stores do not spawn git while existing uninitialized/mid-clone/snapshot behavior stays covered."}],"file_impact_scope":"paths","keyring":[],"last_status_env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","managed_refs":[["relates_to","7063-beed-fa51-4142"]],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"eba9de12-9bce-4752-9b9d-349585725c4c","plan_review_phase":"execution","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":3,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"closed","tags":[],"ticket_id":"d582-e941-96b6-41a8","ticket_type":"task","title":"store_is_usable adds an unthrottled git spawn to every read (~17ms, 4x on a small store)","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["ba838beb-afa7-4bcd-8761-7233e1461b17","2c7d5110-cfde-43f0-b85b-da9a899ac61c","e0423395-bfd4-49da-b29d-102435a19026","196a5151-6ca1-47ae-8254-17b13e78bd78","8f6447d9-e288-4b6f-b493-24307dd7575a","355803cb-288d-47f3-bc5f-4e1a7ef13c46","bf17206d-6117-4a79-8a6c-6d9d6b249bff","a81478f8-3f5d-4a4d-b076-12dffc90d11d","e592273a-0273-4114-bdfa-da15ccb65c28","5960db22-f432-4e2e-ae00-0445ece871f9","eba9de12-9bce-4752-9b9d-349585725c4c","2f910cc5-ea15-4896-90ab-b7659b31eea8"]},"env_id":"","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1788848502372594274,"uuid":"7be8560c-5f4f-444b-8798-52889873c42a"}